{"kind":"assert","text":"Evidence found while filing the phase-2 artifacts, in a neighbouring area and out of scope for this head. Recording it rather than fixing it, so it is not lost and so nobody has to rediscover it.\n\nWriting artifact 2b6e283b2a7c1979a9b30c0ff7dcb2611eb89ae6 landed and folded effective, but the resident could not read the projection back and returned:\n\n  persist verified frontier before returning data: local rollback witness could not advance: rename /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq/.git/gitseq/config.json.tmp /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq/.git/gitseq/config.json: no such file or directory\n\nThe record itself is fine: inspect confirms sequence 6501, effective, not stale. The config file exists and had just been rewritten. What failed is the write-back.\n\nThe cause is that the verified-frontier save writes through one fixed temporary name. internal/app/app.go:509-513 builds MetaDir/config.json.tmp, writes it, and renames it over config.json. The name does not vary by process. With several actors live in this workroom — four codex sessions, four planner sessions, hugh and me were present at the time — two savers overlap, the first rename consumes the shared temporary, and the second gets ENOENT on a file it believed it had just written. Both may also write the same temporary concurrently, so a rename can publish a half-written config; that is the worse of the two outcomes and it is silent.\n\nThis is the same class of defect as the one this request is about, one directory over: a shared mutable name with no owner. The fix is small and local — a per-process temporary name, as PublishResident already does not do either (internal/app/resident.go:41 uses a fixed resident.json.tmp) — but it is a change to the checkpoint and config surface, it belongs to whoever owns that area, and putting it in this head would widen a reviewed scope after approval. It wants its own request.\n\nNo work is blocked. Everything filed in this session was verified effective from the projection rather than from the write response."}